REFLECTION
==========

1. Which observed problem most strongly shaped your design brief, and why?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
The number of filters on the NRCan search form. Nine filter groups show up
at once before you see any results, so it's hard to know where to start.
That's why my first objective is starting a trip with 3 fields or fewer,
and why FuelFare's Home page only asks for From, To and Vehicle.


2. How do your proposed visual design principles support your target users?
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Commuters and road-trippers mostly want one answer: what the trip will
cost. Clarity puts the trip cost as the biggest text on the results page.
Consistency means the same buttons and cards everywhere, so returning
users don't have to relearn anything. Accessibility keeps text readable
and buttons at least 44px, which matters because most people will use it
on a phone.


3. Which colour or contrast decision required revision after testing?
--------------------------------------------------------------------
The primary button hover. I first used red-600 (#C22232), but when I saw
it next to the default button it looked almost the same. I changed it to
red-700 (#A11D2A), which is easy to tell apart and still passes contrast
at 7.71:1. I also changed input borders from #DCDCDC to #4F4F4F because
the light grey was only 1.37:1 and failed for form fields.


4. How did the Design System Studio V4.1 influence your typography or
   colour decisions? Identify something you verified independently in
   Figma or the browser.
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Studio gave me the OKLCH red and neutral ramps from my base red #D72638,
which made it easy to pick hover and background colours that fit
together. It also gave me the type scale and clamp() for headings. I
checked the contrast ratios myself, and the hover problem only showed up
when I looked at the buttons side by side in Figma.


5. Which part of your Figma Style Guide V1 is most reusable, and what
   still needs improvement?
---------------------------------------------------------------------
The named colour and text styles are the most reusable, because every
future screen can use color/primary or type/body without picking values
again. What still needs work: a dark theme, more components like inputs
and error messages, and turning the spacing scale into Figma variables.


6. How will your design brief and style guide guide Milestone 2?
----------------------------------------------------------------
The brief's scope gives me the pages for the sitemap: Home, Calculate
Trip, Trip Results, My Garage and Saved Trips. The objectives decide the
layout, like keeping the calculator at the top of Home on mobile. The
style guide's spacing, type and colour styles will keep the wireframes
consistent across mobile, tablet and desktop.

